Wheel Clamp:
  Wheel clamps/ wheel locks are devices that are clamped onto the wheels of cars. They are intended to prevent the car being moved, often due to illegal parking or
  vehicle repossession.
  Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheel_clamp
  Image source: https://www.yarrasupply.com.au/products/wheel-clamp-13-14-15?srsltid=AfmBOoqbV9Sppl8ziscTUHfojuuwpfVP9_8OR95wT99yxf9CwvXr2NSs
RFID Card Reader:
  Radio Frequency Identification locks use electromagnetic fields to enable communication between two devices, often a card/ fob and a reader. These are often used in
  industry and businesses to provide a secondary layer of security when trying to access a building/ room. The RFID tag communicates with the reader via radio waves.
  The tag must be programmed with a unique credential that will identify and allow access for authorised people. However, there is no guarantee that the person using
  the tag is the same person the tag belongs to.
  Source: https://www.assaabloy.com/au/en/stories/blog/rfid-door-locks--smart-entry-systems
  Image source: cykeorfid.com/rfid-door-lock-access-control-system-the-subtle-power-behind-secure-doors/
Biometric Lock:
  Biometric locks use biometric technology to secure doors. It uses things like fingerprints, voice recognition, retina scans or facial recognition. These are all things
  that are inherant to a person, and cannot be easily replicated by someone trying to gain unauthorised access. These locks don't require remembering any passwords, PINs,
  or keys, just physical, biological features.
  Source: https://www.nordencommunication.com/en/blog/what-is-biometric-smart-lock
  Image source: https://manuals.plus/asin/B08XQ8K41P
Time Lock:
  Commonly used in bank vaults, time locks use a built in timer to prevent vaults from being openned between certain times. For example, they are used in bank vaults
  to secure the vault after-hours, removing the risk of bank managers being kidnapped for vault combinations. 
  Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_lock
  Image source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Timelocks.jpg
Solenoid Lock:
  Solenoid locks contain a solenoid, which is a coil of wire designed to create a magnetic field when current is passed through it. This magnetic field moves the 
  locking bails. Some solenoid locks are default locked, requiring the electirical current to unlock the door. This means that the lock is still secure even if there
  is a power outage.
  Source: https://magnet-schultzamerica.com/resources/blog/whysolenoidspart1/#:~:text=Essentially%2C%20it%20acts%20like%20a,mechanisms%20makes%20them%20particularly%20valuable.
  Image source: https://magnet-schultzamerica.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/lockingsolenoid.png
Maglock:
  Electromagnetic locks use an electrically generated magnetic field to hold the door shut. They magnetically attach to a plate attached to the door. These locks,
  unlike the solenoid lock, requires constant power to generate the magnetic field. Without the electrical current, the magnetic field falters and there is nothing
  actually locking the door. Despite this, they are low energy consumption, so require little amounts of electricity, despite its constant requirement.
  Source: https://kerisys.com/resources/everything-you-need-to-know-about-a-maglock/
  Image source: https://www.locksgalore.com.au/security-products/product/fsh-maglock-fem5000g-magnetic-gate-lock?srsltid=AfmBOoqtkGNbb6js9snqHcvyjJkZW5lzK59Qselszubt-CPC2pxP-2nA
